Summary:
On June 14, 2017, one person was killed and four others wounded in a shooting targeting Republican lawmakers at a baseball field outside Washington, D.C. One gunman — later identified as James Hodgkinson, a 66-year-old home inspector living in the St. Louis, Missouri, area — approached the field and began firing his rifle and sidearm at the group. He exchanged fire with police before being shot. A fifth person suffered an injury and was taken to the hospital. Five other people were injured, including Representative Mo Brooks (R-AL) and three Capitol Hill police officers. All injuries were non-life threatening.
Background: The baseball game between Democratic and Republican lawmakers at the Eugene Simpson Stadium Park in Alexandria, Virginia, was held annually for more than a century. The event was organized by the Congressional Sportsmen’s Foundation, which brings together legislators to enjoy the great outdoors while raising money for nonprofit organizations, such as veteran care and the fight against child hunger. The foundation typically raised $150,000 to $200,000 each year. For the past several years, the game was played in June, which led to speculation that the shootings may have occurred in retaliation for the upcoming vote to repeal Obamacare (also known as the Affordable Care Act).
Injury Information: The injured included:
Representative Steve Scalise (R-LA) – the House majority whip – who was shot in the hip;
Representative Roger Williams (R-TX);
James T. Hodgkinson, who died from injuries sustained during the exchange with police;
Zack Barth, a congressional aide to Representative Roger Williams (R-TX);
Mattew Mika, executive director of government affairs for Tyson Foods Inc.; and
Timothy Corbin, a retired Secret Service agent.
Location: Eugene Simpson Stadium Park in Alexandria, Virginia.
Crime Type: Shooting
